$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 07/Practice
::HC-W7-P01::A district chooses Facebook because staff use it frequently, although the priority audience has low smartphone ownership. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=channel selection based on staff preference#Correct. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
~need for complementary interactive channel#Not the best answer. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
~timing misaligned with action#Not the best answer. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
~messenger-role mismatch#Not the best answer. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
}

::HC-W7-P02::A district chooses Facebook because staff use it frequently, although the priority audience has low smartphone ownership. What is the strongest next response? {
=Choose channels from audience access data and retain offline pathways for those without smartphones.#Correct. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
~Pair broad-reach radio with confidential counselling, hotline or clinic channels.#Not the best answer. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
~Send reminders early enough for transport and planning before the service window.#Not the best answer. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
~Use the expert for evidence and local health workers or community messengers for practical access guidance.#Not the best answer. Internal familiarity is not evidence of audience reach.
}

::HC-W7-P03::A radio campaign creates awareness but listeners need private answers about contraception. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=need for complementary interactive channel#Correct. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
~timing misaligned with action#Not the best answer. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
~messenger-role mismatch#Not the best answer. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
~impressions mistaken for unique reach#Not the best answer. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
}

::HC-W7-P04::A radio campaign creates awareness but listeners need private answers about contraception. What is the strongest next response? {
=Pair broad-reach radio with confidential counselling, hotline or clinic channels.#Correct. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
~Send reminders early enough for transport and planning before the service window.#Not the best answer. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
~Use the expert for evidence and local health workers or community messengers for practical access guidance.#Not the best answer. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
~Use platform definitions and unique-reach estimates where available, and avoid equating impressions with people.#Not the best answer. One channel rarely performs every communication function well.
}

::HC-W7-P05::An SMS reminder for a morning clinic is sent at 7 p.m. after the clinic has closed. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=timing misaligned with action#Correct. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
~messenger-role mismatch#Not the best answer. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
~impressions mistaken for unique reach#Not the best answer. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
~privacy-inappropriate channel#Not the best answer. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
}

::HC-W7-P06::An SMS reminder for a morning clinic is sent at 7 p.m. after the clinic has closed. What is the strongest next response? {
=Send reminders early enough for transport and planning before the service window.#Correct. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
~Use the expert for evidence and local health workers or community messengers for practical access guidance.#Not the best answer. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
~Use platform definitions and unique-reach estimates where available, and avoid equating impressions with people.#Not the best answer. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
~Use channels that protect confidentiality and allow people to seek information without being identified.#Not the best answer. Delivered information is not timely if action is no longer possible.
}

::HC-W7-P07::A national expert is trusted for technical evidence but cannot answer local access questions. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=messenger-role mismatch#Correct. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
~impressions mistaken for unique reach#Not the best answer. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
~privacy-inappropriate channel#Not the best answer. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
~paid media without earned-media preparedness#Not the best answer. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
}

::HC-W7-P08::A national expert is trusted for technical evidence but cannot answer local access questions. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use the expert for evidence and local health workers or community messengers for practical access guidance.#Correct. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
~Use platform definitions and unique-reach estimates where available, and avoid equating impressions with people.#Not the best answer. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
~Use channels that protect confidentiality and allow people to seek information without being identified.#Not the best answer. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
~Prepare spokespeople and key messages so news coverage reinforces rather than contradicts campaign communication.#Not the best answer. Different messengers can credibly perform different roles.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 07/Mastery
::HC-W7-M01::A campaign reports 2 million impressions and concludes it reached 2 million people. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=impressions mistaken for unique reach#Correct. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
~privacy-inappropriate channel#Not the best answer. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
~paid media without earned-media preparedness#Not the best answer. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
~insufficient frequency and sequencing#Not the best answer. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
}

::HC-W7-M02::A campaign reports 2 million impressions and concludes it reached 2 million people. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use platform definitions and unique-reach estimates where available, and avoid equating impressions with people.#Correct. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
~Use channels that protect confidentiality and allow people to seek information without being identified.#Not the best answer. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
~Prepare spokespeople and key messages so news coverage reinforces rather than contradicts campaign communication.#Not the best answer. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
~Plan repetition and message progression based on the decision cycle without creating unnecessary fatigue.#Not the best answer. Repeated exposures can generate multiple impressions per person.
}

::HC-W7-M03::A sensitive HIV message is broadcast through a loudspeaker in a small settlement. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=privacy-inappropriate channel#Correct. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
~paid media without earned-media preparedness#Not the best answer. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
~insufficient frequency and sequencing#Not the best answer. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
~technology-led channel substitution#Not the best answer. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
}

::HC-W7-M04::A sensitive HIV message is broadcast through a loudspeaker in a small settlement. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use channels that protect confidentiality and allow people to seek information without being identified.#Correct. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
~Prepare spokespeople and key messages so news coverage reinforces rather than contradicts campaign communication.#Not the best answer. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
~Plan repetition and message progression based on the decision cycle without creating unnecessary fatigue.#Not the best answer. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
~Compare coverage, data cost and audience use before replacing a high-access local channel.#Not the best answer. Channel visibility can create stigma or disclosure risk.
}

::HC-W7-M05::A media plan buys frequent radio spots but never trains spokespeople for interviews. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=paid media without earned-media preparedness#Correct. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
~insufficient frequency and sequencing#Not the best answer. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
~technology-led channel substitution#Not the best answer. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
~speculation under media pressure#Not the best answer. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
}

::HC-W7-M06::A media plan buys frequent radio spots but never trains spokespeople for interviews. What is the strongest next response? {
=Prepare spokespeople and key messages so news coverage reinforces rather than contradicts campaign communication.#Correct. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
~Plan repetition and message progression based on the decision cycle without creating unnecessary fatigue.#Not the best answer. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
~Compare coverage, data cost and audience use before replacing a high-access local channel.#Not the best answer. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
~State what is confirmed, what is being verified and when an update will be available.#Not the best answer. Channel integration includes responses to earned media.
}

::HC-W7-M07::A message is posted once on several channels and then disappears during a four-week campaign. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=insufficient frequency and sequencing#Correct. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
~technology-led channel substitution#Not the best answer. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
~speculation under media pressure#Not the best answer. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
~redundancy without equity#Not the best answer. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
}

::HC-W7-M08::A message is posted once on several channels and then disappears during a four-week campaign. What is the strongest next response? {
=Plan repetition and message progression based on the decision cycle without creating unnecessary fatigue.#Correct. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
~Compare coverage, data cost and audience use before replacing a high-access local channel.#Not the best answer. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
~State what is confirmed, what is being verified and when an update will be available.#Not the best answer. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
~Use channel redundancy to increase reliability while deliberately extending reach to excluded groups.#Not the best answer. Single exposure may be insufficient for recall or action.
}

::HC-W7-M09::A rural programme replaces community radio with video streaming to modernise the campaign. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=technology-led channel substitution#Correct. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
~speculation under media pressure#Not the best answer. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
~redundancy without equity#Not the best answer. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
~messenger expertise mismatch#Not the best answer. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
}

::HC-W7-M10::A rural programme replaces community radio with video streaming to modernise the campaign. What is the strongest next response? {
=Compare coverage, data cost and audience use before replacing a high-access local channel.#Correct. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
~State what is confirmed, what is being verified and when an update will be available.#Not the best answer. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
~Use channel redundancy to increase reliability while deliberately extending reach to excluded groups.#Not the best answer. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
~Use the musician for attention or social endorsement but link technical claims to qualified, trusted experts.#Not the best answer. Newer technology is not inherently more effective.
}

::HC-W7-M11::A journalist asks about an unconfirmed outbreak figure and the spokesperson guesses to avoid looking uninformed. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=speculation under media pressure#Correct. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
~redundancy without equity#Not the best answer. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
~messenger expertise mismatch#Not the best answer. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
~self-selection in channel feedback#Not the best answer. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
}

::HC-W7-M12::A journalist asks about an unconfirmed outbreak figure and the spokesperson guesses to avoid looking uninformed. What is the strongest next response? {
=State what is confirmed, what is being verified and when an update will be available.#Correct. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
~Use channel redundancy to increase reliability while deliberately extending reach to excluded groups.#Not the best answer. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
~Use the musician for attention or social endorsement but link technical claims to qualified, trusted experts.#Not the best answer. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
~Treat callers as a listening signal and triangulate with representative or broader audience evidence.#Not the best answer. Credibility is stronger when uncertainty is acknowledged than when guesses are presented as facts.
}

::HC-W7-M13::A clinic sends the same message by SMS, WhatsApp and email to the same connected users while offline households receive nothing. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=redundancy without equity#Correct. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
~messenger expertise mismatch#Not the best answer. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
~self-selection in channel feedback#Not the best answer. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
~engagement metric overriding safety#Not the best answer. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
}

::HC-W7-M14::A clinic sends the same message by SMS, WhatsApp and email to the same connected users while offline households receive nothing. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use channel redundancy to increase reliability while deliberately extending reach to excluded groups.#Correct. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
~Use the musician for attention or social endorsement but link technical claims to qualified, trusted experts.#Not the best answer. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
~Treat callers as a listening signal and triangulate with representative or broader audience evidence.#Not the best answer. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
~Assess privacy and safe access before prioritising the channel, even if engagement is lower elsewhere.#Not the best answer. Multiple digital channels can still reach the same narrow segment.
}

::HC-W7-M15::A campaign selects a popular musician to explain complex vaccine safety evidence. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=messenger expertise mismatch#Correct. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
~self-selection in channel feedback#Not the best answer. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
~engagement metric overriding safety#Not the best answer. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
~unsupported channel attribution#Not the best answer. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
}

::HC-W7-M16::A campaign selects a popular musician to explain complex vaccine safety evidence. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use the musician for attention or social endorsement but link technical claims to qualified, trusted experts.#Correct. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
~Treat callers as a listening signal and triangulate with representative or broader audience evidence.#Not the best answer. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
~Assess privacy and safe access before prioritising the channel, even if engagement is lower elsewhere.#Not the best answer. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
~Use channel-specific exposure data or experimental/quasi-experimental comparisons before assigning effect to one channel.#Not the best answer. Popularity and technical credibility are different assets.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 07/Institutional
::HC-W7-I01::Radio call-in questions are used as proof that most residents oppose vaccination. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=self-selection in channel feedback#Correct. People who call are a self-selected subset.
~engagement metric overriding safety#Not the best answer. People who call are a self-selected subset.
~unsupported channel attribution#Not the best answer. People who call are a self-selected subset.
~channel selection based on staff preference#Not the best answer. People who call are a self-selected subset.
}

::HC-W7-I02::Radio call-in questions are used as proof that most residents oppose vaccination. What is the strongest next response? {
=Treat callers as a listening signal and triangulate with representative or broader audience evidence.#Correct. People who call are a self-selected subset.
~Assess privacy and safe access before prioritising the channel, even if engagement is lower elsewhere.#Not the best answer. People who call are a self-selected subset.
~Use channel-specific exposure data or experimental/quasi-experimental comparisons before assigning effect to one channel.#Not the best answer. People who call are a self-selected subset.
~Choose channels from audience access data and retain offline pathways for those without smartphones.#Not the best answer. People who call are a self-selected subset.
}

::HC-W7-I03::The highest-engagement social platform is chosen for a domestic-violence referral campaign even though users commonly share devices. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=engagement metric overriding safety#Correct. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
~unsupported channel attribution#Not the best answer. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
~channel selection based on staff preference#Not the best answer. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
~need for complementary interactive channel#Not the best answer. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
}

::HC-W7-I04::The highest-engagement social platform is chosen for a domestic-violence referral campaign even though users commonly share devices. What is the strongest next response? {
=Assess privacy and safe access before prioritising the channel, even if engagement is lower elsewhere.#Correct. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
~Use channel-specific exposure data or experimental/quasi-experimental comparisons before assigning effect to one channel.#Not the best answer. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
~Choose channels from audience access data and retain offline pathways for those without smartphones.#Not the best answer. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
~Pair broad-reach radio with confidential counselling, hotline or clinic channels.#Not the best answer. The best metric depends on the communication objective and risk.
}

::HC-W7-I05::A campaign changes many channels at once and service uptake rises, so the team credits television specifically. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unsupported channel attribution#Correct. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
~channel selection based on staff preference#Not the best answer. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
~need for complementary interactive channel#Not the best answer. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
~timing misaligned with action#Not the best answer. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
}

::HC-W7-I06::A campaign changes many channels at once and service uptake rises, so the team credits television specifically. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use channel-specific exposure data or experimental/quasi-experimental comparisons before assigning effect to one channel.#Correct. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
~Choose channels from audience access data and retain offline pathways for those without smartphones.#Not the best answer. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
~Pair broad-reach radio with confidential counselling, hotline or clinic channels.#Not the best answer. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
~Send reminders early enough for transport and planning before the service window.#Not the best answer. A multi-channel campaign does not reveal which channel caused change without additional design.
}

